% \section{Introduction}
%
% This package provides short macros for a wide range of physical units: the
% SI base and derived units, all of the SI prefixes, cgs and Gaussian units,
% astronomical units, and many imperial, US customary and historical units.
% Each unit macro enforces math mode and sets the unit in upright (roman)
% type, preceded by a small space that separates it from the number, so
% \verb|3\m| gives 3\m.
%
% \textsf{physunits} has some overlap with \textsf{siunitx} but uses different
% macro names and a much lighter syntax. The two packages are generally 
% compatible, but there is risk of clashes due to the definitions of
% the SI unit prefixes. In my testing, both packages worked correctly together.
%
% \section{Obtaining the package}\label{sec:obtaining}
% \changes{v1.3.0}{2026/11/24}{Added section on where to obtain the package.}
%
% The current release of \textsf{physunits} is available from CTAN,
% \url{https://ctan.org/pkg/physunits}, and from the GitHub repository,
% \url{https://github.com/astrobit/physunits}. The repository also holds the
% current development version, which may include changes not yet released
% to CTAN.
%
